00:13:57 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You've just made the most important point in this entire conversation. I want to address it head-on, because you're right that if my explanation lands as "there's nothing you can do, so just give in to terror," that is a genuinely dangerous message. So let me be absolutely clear: I am not saying there's nothing you can mentally do. I am saying the *goal* of what you're trying to do with your mind shifts depending on the phase of the attack. The mental game is never irrelevant. It just changes from a steering wheel to a life jacket. Let me reframe everything around that, because your concern is exactly why doctors, nurses, and respiratory therapists say "stay calm" in the first place. ### What You Can Always Mentally Do (The Life Jacket) Even in the worst, chemically-driven attack where willpower can't open the airways, your mind is still the most powerful tool you have. The critical distinction is: you are not using it to directly reverse the spasm. You are using it to do three absolutely life-saving things that prevent the attack from killing you before the medication can work. 1. **You prevent the lethal spiral of panic-asphyxiation.** The sensation of suffocation triggers a primal panic that makes you gasp and fight. This burns through oxygen at a terrifying rate, tires your respiratory muscles to the point of failure, and increases the metabolic demand for oxygen you can't get. Staying calm isn't a cure, but it is a metabolic brake. It buys you time. It keeps you from drowning yourself faster. This is not "nothing." This is buying minutes that your rescue inhaler needs to work. 2. **You stay in control of your voluntary actions.** The person who panics mindlessly might not be able to find their inhaler, coordinate the steps to use a spacer, or call for help. The person who uses mental discipline to stay calm can physically go through the motions: sit upright, find the medication, shake the canister, breathe out, seal their lips, spray